<headroom-library version="1" id="crunch-south-side-bite" category="Crunch" order="30" instrument="guitar" style="Chicago Blues">
  <description>A small 1x12 tweed combo turned nearly all the way up, no pedals: a raw, honky midrange that barks like an amplified harp, with a little room around it. Fingers or a light pick; back the guitar volume off for rhythm, dig in to make it snarl.</description>
  <tags><tag>Crunch</tag><tag>Blues</tag><tag>Chicago Blues</tag><tag>Tweed</tag><tag>No pedals</tag><tag>Rhythm</tag><tag>Lead</tag></tags>
  <sources>
    <source url="https://blog.truefire.com/guitar-lessons/chicago-blues-guitar-tone-with-jeffery-marshall/" title="TrueFire: Chicago blues guitar tone with Jeffery Marshall"/>
    <source url="https://www.guitarworld.com/features/classic-gear-fender-tweed-deluxe" title="Guitar World: Fender 'Tweed' Deluxe, history of the definitive tweed amp"/>
    <source url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fender_Deluxe" title="Fender Deluxe (Wikipedia)"/>
    <source url="https://www.ampbooks.com/mobile/classic-circuits/fender-deluxe-5e3/" title="Ampbooks: Fender Deluxe 5E3 circuit analysis"/>
    <source url="https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/hubert-sumlin-blues-guitar-pioneer" title="Guitar World: Learn the guitar stylings of the pioneering Hubert Sumlin"/>
    <source url="https://chicagoreader.com/music/jimmy-rogers-blues-guitar-muddy-chess/" title="Chicago Reader: Jimmy Rogers played a part in every era of Chicago blues"/>
